response:
{
  "answer": "Yes",
  "reasoning": "If you reasonably suspect the person (even if it’s your parent) is taking property that does not belong to them, you should report the suspected theft — but do so after taking sensible, proportionate steps. First, verify facts if you can do so safely: confirm whose tools they are, whether they had permission, and whether there is an innocent explanation. Avoid a risky confrontation at night. If you cannot verify and you still suspect unauthorized removal, document what you observed (time, location, descriptions, photos only if legal/safe) and notify the property owner or building manager. If the situation appears to be an ongoing or violent crime, call the police immediately. If it’s a first-time, potentially recoverable misunderstanding, consider talking privately with your parent first. In short: report suspected theft to the appropriate party when you have reasonable grounds or when safety or evidence concerns require it, but prioritize confirming basic facts and your own safety before escalating."
}

